good afternoon and welcome to today's  presentation on how to use machen in  clinical practice we will go through an  overview of Megan and then some  searching and navigation tips we will  also summarize some ways to incorporate  Megan in the clinical genetics process  let's start by showing you the Megan  homepage in our URL you can also find us  by googling NIH Majan Megan is a free  website where you can find information  about genetic phenotypes aggregated from  many authoritative sources so instead of  checking if there is a gene reviews for  a condition then opening in your browser  to read what form M has to say and then  go into clinical trials to look for  relevant clinical trials for your  patients you can come to imagine and in  one page see a summary of the  information each of these resources has  on the condition and links to those  resources so imagine you will find  information on Mendelian disorders  pharmacogenetic responses complex  diseases with a genetic component and  clinical features each measure and  record has a disease name the preferred  name is nomad CT which is the source of  disease names most electronic medical  records use so it should facilitate  incorporation in clinical settings you  will also see other attributes of the  disease such as alternate names from  different sources with their identifier  z' again the purpose being to easily  integrate match and in electronic  medical systems in your hospital or  clinic you can see causative genes mode  of inheritance in disease descriptions  along with the list of clinical features  you get access to patient oriented  resources like genetics home reference  guard and MedlinePlus in the same page  provides access to actionable  information such as practice guidelines  clinical trials.gov a list of genetic  tests for the disease in the GTR in the  list of variants in the causative genes  in green bar so these are the resources  you can find in machen and then some  more  so instead of opening it up to check the  latest publications from PubMed or  searching professional guidelines for  guidelines of professional societies for  guidelines you can find them all imagine  okay so now let's go over how to use  Majan this is the imagine home page  there are links here to documentation  that describes in detail what Megan is  and how to use it  Megan serves as the phenotype source for  submissions to clinvar in GTR for  information on how to use it for this  purpose please look at the presentation  available from this home page I want to  point out that Megan store's  professional guidelines and we provide  the link to all guidelines from here we  currently have 455 professional  guidelines that range from practice  guidelines from medical professional  societies like ACMG and SGC and NH CCN  all the way to FDA drug labels for the  drugs with pharmacogenetic information  imagine is a resource for you and we  want to be useful to you so we welcome  your feedback and if you see we are  missing a guideline please contact us  and we will add it there is also a link  to the FTP site which has files with our  data that you can download for free you  can search imagine by entering your  search term in the search box you can  use things like these names drug  responses genes even Oh mum numbers and  HBO IDs one feature of madryn is that  you can search for conditions by a  clinical feature we draw from data from  HBO and mmm in soon we will be adding  orphanet as well to strengthen these  disease to clinical feature  relationships so if you search for  arachnid actally the top result is the  record for the clinical feature but you  also have a link at the very top of the  page if you want to see all the Z's that  have arachnid actually as a clinical  feature so clicking on the clinical  feature record  you get the preferred name whenever  available you will see an image from any  GRI elements of morphology a definition  with a source in this case it's HPL  below that you get a list of conditions  with a clinical feature again these come  from mappings between omen and hbl when  you hover over the condition you get a  summary of the disease and the link that  will take you to the full record for the  disease you can see in this example for  Marfan syndrome okay so in the Advanced  Search located just below the search box  machen allows you to search by  specifying multiple parameters like  clinical features genes chromosomal  locations mode of inheritance or umem  numbers for example you can specify two  different clinical features by selecting  clinical feature in the search builder  then type a term and click the show  index list to select from a set of  predefined terms you can add the gene or  anything else you know about your  patient and then click search now this  is a search result page which lists the  diseases that fit your parameters this  page shows you the summary of the  disease so you can see if that is the  disease you are looking for before you  even open a page you also see various  useful links at the bottom a link to  list of tests in GTR for this condition  link to a list of variants on the  causative genes in clinvar link to the  gene record in NCBI gene database link  to almond record and a link to gene  reviews when available now let's look at  what a disease record looks like in  Metron this is the disease record for  Marfan syndrome these pages are along so  you just scroll down to see all the  information or you can use the table of  contents on the right hand side to jump  to the section of interest view at the  top you have the disease  name in synonyms mode of inheritance  causative genes identifier with links to  sources like Coleman and orphanet then  you have a list of descriptions from  available sources like gene reviews on  genetics home reference HBO or human  phenotype ontology typically medical  genetic summaries and pharmgkb have  definitions for pharmacogenetic  phenotypes NCI the National Cancer  Institute for oncology phenotypes or any  other resource with disease information  we are able to access for um you also  have links to go look at the full page  for the disease from the source so you  have one disease description at the top  and then if there are descriptions from  other sources they will be list listed  in the additional descriptions section  along with links to those sources so  below that you have a list of clinical  features for this condition again from  data aggregators from mm and HBO when  you hover over the clinical feature you  see a description in two links one for  the full record as you saw before for  arachnid actally and want to get a list  of diseases with the clinical feature  now you can use this list of clinical  features to guide your evaluation of  your patient you know that you have the  term hierarchy these may come from NCBI  or this way for mesh or or finatus  labeled below are the professional  guidelines in suggested articles these  are manually curated by our staff then  you see a list of recent publications  from PubMed categorized by etiology  diagnosis therapy prognosis clinical  prediction guidelines and systematic  reviews basically you get a PubMed  search right on the mesh on page to  supplement all this information the  right-hand side of each page provides  links to even more resources based on  conditions and genes for example you  have links to genetic tests registered  in GTR by test  methodology or the co-option links to  clinical trials.gov  for a list of clinical trials for the  condition you are researching links to  molecular resources like the omen gene  pages in clinvar links to gene reviews  when available and medical genetics  summaries for pharmacogenetic records  links to PubMed health for information  on the disease and clinical features  links to consumer resources like card  and ghr to educate your patient and  their families and these are just to  name a few merchants serves as the  phenotype source for GTR and clinvar  this enables us to standardize disease  names across tests from all labs as well  as standardized phenotypes in variant  interpretation in clinvar all our data  is freely available for download as  mentioned before because Megan  aggregates phenotype data from different  sources and preserves the source  identifiers it can be integrated in  electronic medical record software to  standardize genetic disease names in the  community hospital systems can use NCBI  API to tailor views of the genetic  information for their users at point of  care in the note about data updates in  Megan Megan processes updates from  sources as they become available not all  sources have the same schedule to  release updates so we process them when  we get them mostly in automated  workflows so in summary you can use  mention to research a phenotype and  access all resources from one page it  can be your one-stop shop for genetic  disease information it can help suggest  conditions that feed the cluster of  clinical features for a patient it  provides the list of clinical features  you can use to evaluate a patient for a  suspected diagnosis you can access  practice guidelines for a condition as  well as therapeutic recommendations for  drug responses based on genotypes from  various professional societies links to  available genetic tests to confirm diet  Yelling's to clinvar to help with  variant interpretation of a test result  links to consumer resources for the  patients and families we want to hear  from you about how to be more useful so  please contact us with your feedback in  any questions you may have at the email  on this slide please note that it's  Meghan and their score help and please  follow us on Twitter for updates thank  you for your attention and enjoy Meghan  there's a couple of questions in here  now  Izzie fetch so this is one of the  utilities is this enabled for Meghan  records and I think the particular  question has to do with whether the Floy  certainly you can get the summary  because it's an entree but I don't know  whether you can get the full XML record  for imagine or not yeah and we can  confirm that and put that in the  follow-up document so another question  is do these medgen records link to gos  data for complex diseases so for example  what are the significant snips  associated with a particular disease so  right now no Megan does not link to G  was data we do link to clean var which  has the G was data there Melissa  Landrum chimed in and said that a key  fetch in fact is not available for  medgen so you're not going to be able to  get the full records that way for Megan  another question is does legend use an  ontology like sno-med CT yes so sno-med  CT is what we select as our preferred  names so we have the sno-med CT  preferred name as well as the  identifiers so yes and Jennifer Lee asks  could you fetch be enabled for Megan  well hopefully yes so it is a matter of  you know getting the resources to do it  but could it be navels yes in the future  and thank you for that suggestion I will  definitely put it on the list okay  here's another question does the end of  national guidelines clearing  affect the curated guidelines mentioned  the funding to support the national  guideline Clearinghouse has not been  secured beyond July 16 2018 right so for  the professional guidelines we do have  the ones from the clearinghouse so the  ones that are going to be archived we  will have them available but they are  not our only source of guidelines so we  will have them  we will have guidelines from other  sources as well since they are not our  only source I'm hoping they doesn't  affect us all too much okay thanks  another question is it common for  hospitals medical centers to integrate  medgen in their health records so are  part of the effort called open info  button so we do have every likely to  have our data available and like I said  this is a free resource we are at NCBI  so everything we have is free now that I  know of there hasn't been any hospital  that has integrated this information  into their system so if you do know of  one I would love to know about it but we  do have everything it's freely available  so any hospital would be able to  integrate it and then depending on how  hospitals or clinics have that  information because we know some  hospitals might might have information  based on HBO IDs or maybe even sno-med  CT Zoar orphanet IDs or whatever it is  we do have all of that information to to  make the the integration smooth and easy  okay thanks everybody I don't see any  question any more questions so let's go  ahead and close the webinar is there  anything else that you need it's Adriano  okay so ok thanks everybody

